// tests/kernel/pci/t14_g4_fixture.pdx — R22-M6-002 (#871) placeholder
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Status
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// GATED ON HARDWARE. This file is a compilable placeholder until real
// PCI configuration-space blobs are captured from a live Thinkpad T14
// Gen 4 (Raptor Lake). See:
//   - tools/capture-t14-g4-pci.md — the operator-side capture recipe
//     (lspci -vvv + per-device dd from /sys/bus/pci/devices/*/config
//     under a Linux boot on the target laptop, with Intel VMD OFF).
//   - tests/kernel/pci/fixtures/t14g4/README.md — the expected on-disk
//     layout of the captured config-*.bin blobs this module will consume.
//   - tests/kernel/acpi/t14_g4_fixture.pdx — the ACPI-static-table
//     sibling; both witnesses fire together once first-light lands.
//
// The R22.M6 close pattern is: the capture doc + fixture README +
// placeholder land together; the config blobs themselves and the
// enabling of this witness follow after the first hardware capture.
// That capture is not blocked on R22 — it will happen alongside R23+
// bring-up when the operator has serial capture + the R19 stub can
// boot to kernel_main on the T14 G4.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Acceptance criteria (what this file will do once real fixtures land)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// When tests/kernel/pci/fixtures/t14g4/config-*.bin exist, this module
// is rewritten to:
//
//   1. Embed each config-*.bin as a byte array (via a tools/ .S wrapper
//      using GNU as .incbin, mirroring tools/ap_trampoline_embed.S — or
//      paideia-as @include_bytes once #1013 lands).
//   2. Provide @pub witness `t14g4_pci_fixture_witness() -> u64` that:
//        - Seeds an ECAM-shim window at a well-known VA pointing at the
//          embedded config blobs (so pci_config_read_u32 hits the fixture
//          bytes instead of live MMIO).
//        - Drives pci_enumerate_all(0) — expects the enumerator to
//          record N devices matching the T14 G4 U/P inventory (VMD off).
//        - For each expected device, walks pci_walk_caps + pcie_walk_ext_caps
//          against the fixture bytes and asserts the cap-chain sequence
//          matches the recorded ground truth.
//        - Asserts BAR sizing via pci_bar_size against the recorded
//          type-0 header decode results.
//        - Emits UART markers `PCI T14G4 OK\n\0` or `PCI T14G4 FAIL\n\0`.
//   3. Is invoked from kernel_main_uefi behind a machine_id-gated
//      dispatch (same MACHINE_ID_T14G4 gate the ACPI fixture uses; a
//      QEMU boot does not exercise this witness).
//
// The expected-values table is populated after the first capture; the
// R22 closure retrospective (design/round-retrospectives/r22-closure.md
// §"Preflight for R23") lists the fields the operator must record when
// the capture happens.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Compilation contract for the R22 close
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This module MUST compile clean under tools/build.sh so the fixture
// harness slot is reserved in the build graph. It emits two marker
// strings and reserves the expected-values slot — no runtime effect,
// no linker symbol other than the marker byte arrays and the reserved
// u64 array. The @pub visibility is intentional so a future consumer
// (or a GDB `x/s` probe) can observe the reservation.

module T14G4Fixture = structure {

  // Marker strings the future witness will emit on the UART. Reserved
  // here so the byte pattern is greppable in kernel.elf even before the
  // witness body lands.

  pub let t14g4_pci_fixture_ok_msg   : [u8; 14] = "PCI T14G4 OK\n\0"
  pub let t14g4_pci_fixture_fail_msg : [u8; 16] = "PCI T14G4 FAIL\n\0"

  // Slot for the eventual expected-values table (populated after first
  // capture). Sized for 64 u64 fields — headroom for ~15 top-level
  // devices × 4 slots each (BDF-packed, vid_did, class_rev, bar0
  // sample) plus meta (device_count, cap_chain_lengths[N]). Byte-zero
  // until real fixtures land.

  pub let mut _t14g4_pci_expected : [u64; 64] = uninit @align(8)
}
